1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,000
This is Task 8, pipeline as code and deployment.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:15,000
A Jenkinsfile describes the whole build and deployment process as code, stored in the repository next to the application.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:17,000
This is the top of ours.

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:27,000
The comment shows the flow: checkout, install, static checks, tests, Selenium, package, build images, publish, deploy and health check.

5
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:36,000
And the key rule, the quality gate: if any command fails, its stage fails and every later stage is skipped.

6
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:42,000
These are the pipeline parameters.

7
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:47,000
They configure the target environment without changing the code.

8
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:49,000
APP ENV chooses staging or prod.

9
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:52,000
BRANCH chooses which Git branch to build.

10
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:58,000
REGISTRY chooses where images are published, by default the local registry.

11
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:02,000
Below is the trigger: poll SCM every five minutes.

12
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:11,000
A GitHub webhook would be faster, but GitHub cannot reach a Jenkins running on localhost, so Jenkins asks Git for new commits instead.

13
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Options: timestamps in the log, only the last twenty builds are kept, no two builds run at the same time because two deployments would fight over the same containers, and a thirty minute time limit.

14
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:39,000
Environment variables: the Python version, and the host port, which is 8082 for prod and 8081 for staging, decided by the parameter.

15
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:48,000
The compose project name includes the environment, so staging and prod get separate containers and separate database volumes.

16
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Stage one, checkout.

17
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:56,000
Jenkins gets the source from Git.

18
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:10,000
The next line deletes the reports and dist folders from the previous build, because the workspace is reused, and an old test report must never be published as the result of the new build.

19
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:18,000
Then it builds the version: the VERSION file plus the short commit id, for example 1.1.0 dash 1c2103c.

20
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:27,000
That one tag is used for the source archive, both images and the health check, so every deployment traces back to one commit.

21
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:34,000
Stage two installs dependencies.

22
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:42,000
It creates a fresh Python virtual environment and installs the pinned requirements, so the build is reproducible.

23
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:53,000
Stage three, static checks, runs compile all, which finds syntax errors, and flake8, which finds style problems, unused imports and undefined names.

24
00:02:53,000 --> 00:03:00,000
These are the build and lint steps for a Python project, and the equivalent of compile in Java.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:02,000
This is not a Maven build.

26
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:13,000
Stage four runs the unit and integration tests with pytest and writes a JUnit XML report.

27
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:17,000
Stage five runs the Selenium browser tests in headless Chromium.

28
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:20,000
Each is just a shell command.

29
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:32,000
If pytest finds a failing test, it exits with an error code, the stage turns red, and Jenkins skips everything after it: packaging, images, publishing and deployment.

30
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:37,000
That is the quality gate, and it is why untested code can never be deployed.

31
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:44,000
Stage six packages the source.

32
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:51,000
Git archive creates a tar gz file with exactly the committed files of this commit, nothing uncommitted.

33
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:59,000
Then sha256 sum writes a checksum file next to it, so anyone can verify later that the archive is unchanged.

34
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:02,000
Both files are archived with the build.

35
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:05,000
This is the source artifact of our pipeline.

36
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:14,000
Stage seven builds two Docker images, the application and the Nginx proxy.

37
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:19,000
Both are tagged with the registry prefix and the version and commit tag.

38
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:27,000
The application image receives the version and the commit as build arguments, so the running container can report them on the health address.

39
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:34,000
Stage eight publishes the images.

40
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:40,000
With the local registry, localhost colon 5000, a simple docker push is enough.

41
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:49,000
For a remote registry such as Docker Hub, the pipeline uses a Jenkins credential, and the password is masked in the log.

42
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:52,000
I must be honest: only the local registry was tested.

43
00:04:52,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Docker Hub is supported by the code but was never run, because it needs an account and a credential.

44
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:06,000
Stage nine deploys.

45
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:13,000
The secret key is injected from a Jenkins credential, only for this block, and masked in the log.

46
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:18,000
Docker compose pull fetches exactly the images that were just published.

47
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:25,000
Docker compose up with dash dash wait starts the containers and blocks until they are healthy.

48
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:29,000
If they never become healthy within two minutes, the stage fails.

49
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:33,000
Deployment uses the tested image, never a rebuild.

50
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:39,000
Stage ten, the health check.

51
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:46,000
It calls the health address through the whole chain, Nginx, Gunicorn and SQLite, and saves the answer.

52
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:55,000
Then two grep commands check that the version and the commit reported by the running application are exactly the ones this build created.

53
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:58,000
If a wrong version were running, this stage would fail.

54
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:02,000
This is how we know that what is deployed is what was tested.

55
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:16,000
Finally, a deployment record is written: environment, branch, version, commit, image names, the address and the Jenkins build address.

56
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:29,000
After every build, success or failure, the post section publishes the test results and archives reports, failure screenshots, the source archive and the record.

57
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:32,000
Jenkins then shows pass and fail trends.

58
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:35,000
A failure message says that nothing was deployed.

59
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:44,000
To summarise, here are the stages with their line numbers.

60
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:48,000
The tests come before package, images, publish and deploy.

61
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:52,000
Deployment happens only after the quality gates pass.

62
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:57,000
The next part of this video shows a real run of this pipeline in Jenkins.

63
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:04,000
Now a real run.

64
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:07,000
In Jenkins I choose Build with Parameters.

65
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:27,000
The form shows the three parameters of the Jenkinsfile with their defaults: APP ENV staging, so it deploys to the staging environment on port eight thousand eighty one; BRANCH develop, the integration branch; and REGISTRY localhost colon five thousand, the local registry.

66
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:28,000
I click Build.

67
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:33,000
This is the stage view of the new build, filling in live.

68
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:38,000
Stage one, checkout, fetches the commit and builds the version and commit tag.

69
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:43,000
Stage two installs the dependencies in a fresh virtual environment.

70
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:48,000
Stage three, static checks, compiles the code and runs the linter.

71
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:52,000
Stage four runs the unit and integration tests.

72
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:56,000
If any of these turns red, everything after it is skipped.

73
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:02,000
Stage five runs the Selenium browser tests in headless Chromium, about twenty seconds.

74
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:06,000
Stage six packages the source archive and writes its checksum.

75
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:14,000
Stage seven builds the two Docker images, the application and Nginx, tagged with the version and the commit.

76
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:18,000
Stage eight publishes both images to the local registry.

77
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:21,000
Only code that passed every test gets this far.

78
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:34,000
Stage nine deploys: Docker compose pulls the published images and starts the containers, and waits until they are healthy.

79
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:42,000
Stage ten, the health check, asks the running application for its version and commit and compares them with this build.

80
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:45,000
When the whole row is green, the build is a success.

81
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:47,000
I wait for the build to finish.

82
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:52,000
The console of the new build confirms each step.

83
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:58,000
Docker build created the application image, tagged with the registry, the version and the commit.

84
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:00,000
Docker push published it.

85
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:07,000
Docker compose up with the wait option started the containers, and Docker reported them healthy.

86
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:13,000
Finally the pipeline printed deployed, with the version, commit tag and the staging address.

87
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:18,000
Every deployment writes a record, archived with the build.

88
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:28,000
It says the environment, the branch, the version, the commit, the image tag, the names of both images, the address, and the Jenkins build address.

89
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:35,000
This is the traceability: from a running staging container back to one commit and one build.
